WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.600
Speaker 1: Student loan payments have&nbsp;
been suspended once again by the Biden&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:07.140
administration as they have announced that&nbsp;
they're going to push back when people will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:13.200
have to start making payments on their bills yet&nbsp;
again. The moratorium started in spring of 2020,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:18.720
of course, with the COVID 19 pandemic and the&nbsp;
plan that President Biden unveiled earlier this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:23.520
year was that in exchange for the $10,000 of&nbsp;
student loan forgiveness. More if you got Pell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:28.860
Grants that they would bring back the payments&nbsp;
at the start of 2023. But then we learned that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:33.540
there were these Republican efforts to challenge&nbsp;
Biden's executive order to forgive the debt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:39.000
and two of the lawsuits ended up succeeding, at&nbsp;
least for now, putting on halt the Biden debt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:43.680
relief plan and leaving millions of Americans in&nbsp;
limbo. The Biden administration is saying that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:50.880
they will resume payments either halfway through&nbsp;
2023 or shortly after the courts ideally allow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:55.680
his executive order to stand. So depending&nbsp;
on what the courts decide to do is when you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.680 --> 00:01:01.260
may have to start making payments again if that&nbsp;
type of thing applies to you. Biden is rightfully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:05.460
playing politics with this, of course, saying&nbsp;
that if Republicans are going to tie up what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:09.240
he wants to do in the courts, he's going to use&nbsp;
his power to make sure that, at least for now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:14.160
people with student loans are benefiting from&nbsp;
his presidency. It would also be kind of strange,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:19.740
unfair if someone who has, let's say, $9,000&nbsp;
worth of student loan debt all of a sudden has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:24.420
to start making payments on that balance again,&nbsp;
even though they thought that they wouldn't have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:27.720
to pay it at all. They thought it was all going&nbsp;
to get forgiven. And now they still don't even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:32.040
know if it's going to ultimately get forgiven.&nbsp;
As I mentioned, there are two cases holding up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:37.800
the debt relief. The first came to us on November&nbsp;
10th after a right wing federal judge in Texas put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:43.980
a halt on the debt relief plan because they were&nbsp;
going to hear out the case of two borrowers who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:49.320
didn't qualify for the debt relief. The plaintiffs&nbsp;
were, of course, backed by conservative groups.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:53.580
And then shortly after that, we heard from the&nbsp;
U.S. Court of Appeals in Missouri. They granted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:59.040
an injunction by six red states against the debt&nbsp;
relief plan. That one has to do with whether Biden&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:04.080
has the authority to do this as president or if&nbsp;
he needs congressional approval. And I got to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:08.220
it pains me to say this, but there is a good&nbsp;
chance, at least a decent chance that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:14.460
courts will end up striking this down outright,&nbsp;
because the legal justification that Biden is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:20.580
using to forgive the debt is kind of on shaky&nbsp;
grounds, like the basis is the 2003 Heroes Act,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:26.640
which allows the Education Department leeway&nbsp;
for rules surrounding student loans if there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:32.340
a war or national emergency. In this case, the&nbsp;
national emergency is being cited as COVID 19,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:37.680
which no doubt would have been successful maybe&nbsp;
back in 2020, 2021, during the worst parts of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:42.000
the pandemic. But the courts, and especially&nbsp;
these right wing courts, may say that, okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:47.820
well, we're beyond COVID as an emergency. It's one&nbsp;
thing to have a pause on payments during a crisis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:51.900
especially when people can't work. It's a&nbsp;
different thing altogether to forgive debt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:56.640
going forward. And so you really need Congress to&nbsp;
pass this. The president can't do this on their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:01.620
own. There's also the question about whether this&nbsp;
will hurt Republicans if they're able to torpedo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:06.600
this thing, because you may imagine that's there&nbsp;
going to be plenty of Republican constituents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:10.980
who would stand to benefit from student loan&nbsp;
forgiveness. Maybe they'll look the other way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:14.580
Maybe they'll say that they didn't want it in&nbsp;
the first place and it was unfair or whatever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:18.840
that type of thing happens. You never know with&nbsp;
these Republican constituents. It's also a long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:23.700
time before they have to vote against it. It'll&nbsp;
be another two years before the 2024 elections,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:27.540
so maybe there won't be any sort of consequences,&nbsp;
but hopefully it doesn't come to that. Hopefully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:32.940
this debt relief plan is going to be able to stand&nbsp;
up because $10,000, up to $20,000, that can really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:37.500
do a lot of good for a lot of people. But let&nbsp;
me know what you think. Will this be allowed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:42.480
to stand up? And if it is struck down, will&nbsp;
Republicans have to pay a political price for it?

